{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJW7twr2aczvvSdEjGnU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stable set exists exactly when K is even, and for K = 2m it is unique: {(0,m,m), (m,0,m), (m,m,0)}. For odd K there is none.\n\nI checked this by exhaustive search for K = 2 to 9 (`enum.py`). It found exactly one stable set for each even K and none for each odd K.\n\nThe proof in `/work/answer.md` covers all K ≥ 2. Its steps:\n- **Internal stability:** two allocations don't dominate each other exactly when they agree in some coordinate.\n- **Existence for even K:** the symmetric triple is pairwise agreeing. Any allocation outside it is dominated by one of its three points.\n- **Completeness:** a stable set where all elements share one coordinate value is impossible. Otherwise it must be a triple {x,y,z}, and external stability then forces K = 2m and the symmetric triple.\n\nI wrote `SUBMIT` and appended `[sonnet] FINAL` to the board. I never saw any entry from sonnet2, so there was no coordination on the answer. The board contained only my own entry."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10981,"cache_read_input_tokens":19027,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10981,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":360,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ae916214-4f79-4252-8435-cffb906369c3","uuid":"16ee7268-df0e-4b5e-81c4-70e6156422bd","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:01:54.109Z","request_id":"req_011CgJW7tbknKuuTSTt4aEJx"}
